---
/*
 * WORDMARK — the definitive LRF lockup in the chrome (P12 / D-84).
 *
 * WHAT WAS WRONG, as arithmetic rather than as taste. The master `assets/logoLRF.png` is 1672×941 but its
 * ink occupies only the middle 998×396 of that canvas — a 2.520:1 stacked lockup surrounded by transparent
 * padding. The previous integration published the master as-is and recovered the lockup with a CSS crop:
 * `width: 166.9%; left: -33.45%` inside an `overflow: hidden` box of 192×64. The horizontal half of that
 * was right; the vertical half was never checked. A 192-wide box shows the ink at 78.6px tall, and the box
 * was 64px — so the browser clipped 7px off the top of the monogram and 7px off the bottom of the word
 * FOTOGRAFIE, on every page. MEASURED on the built site at 1440×900: box 192×64, image rendered
 * 320.4×180.3 at top −58.2px. It is not a rendering quirk; the box ratio was 3.000 and the artwork's is
 * 2.520.
 *
 * THE FIX IS AT THE ASSET AND THE LAYOUT, NOT AT THE ARTWORK. `scripts/make-brand-assets.mjs` trims the
 * master to its own ink once, at build-authoring time, and writes `/brand/lrf-lockup.png` (440×175, 26 KB
 * against the master's 136 KB). The component then needs no crop, no offset and no overflow at all: the
 * element carries the artwork's own `aspect-ratio`, the image fills it, and clipping is structurally
 * impossible — there is no box for the artwork to be larger than. `object-fit: contain` is the belt to
 * that brace: if a future asset ever arrives at a different ratio it letterboxes instead of distorting.
 * The mark is never stretched, never filtered, never given a background; it is the owner's file, whole.
 *
 * SIZE. The lockup is stacked, so its height is what the 4.75rem chrome bar has to accommodate — and the
 * bar is deliberately NOT made taller for it (that would be a header redesign, and every page's rhythm is
 * measured against this bar). At 9.75rem wide the whole lockup is 61.9px tall inside a 76px bar: ~7px of
 * clearance top and bottom, which reads as intentional rather than as a mark jammed against two edges.
 * Verified in the render at 320/360/390/430/768/1024/1280/1440/1920.
 *
 * D-88 — THE MARK REPLACES THE LOCKUP (owner instruction, 2026-09-13). The owner supplied the LRF monogram
 * over its gold rule WITHOUT the two name lines (`assets/logoLRF-beperkt.png`), to be used as the logo across
 * the whole site. The construction above is kept exactly: trimmed derivative `/brand/lrf-mark.png`, the
 * artwork's own ratio on the box, `object-fit: contain`. Only the size changes, because
 * the mark is a different shape: at the lockup's 62px height the bare monogram would be ~2.5× as large as it
 * was inside the lockup and would dominate the chrome. It is sized so the letterforms stay in the range they
 * had: 6–6.75rem wide = 43–49px tall inside the 76px bar. The accessible name still carries the full
 * business name, which the artwork itself no longer spells out.
 *
 * D-89 (2026-09-18, owner instruction) — NEW MASTER. The owner supplied a revised full lockup
 * (`assets/logoLRF-2026-09-18.png`, same 1672×941 canvas as before, larger FOTOGRAFIE line) to become the
 * site's logo. `make-brand-assets.mjs` re-measured the monogram+rule crop from the new file: 514×232 ink
 * (ratio 2.2155, imperceptibly different from D-88's 511×231). Old masters kept, superseded, not deleted
 * (CLAUDE.md §9.3).
 *
 * D-90 (2026-09-18, owner instruction, same day) — THE FULL LOCKUP RETURNS TO THE HEADER AND FOOTER. D-89
 * first kept the header monogram-only (the owner's answer when asked). The owner then looked at the live
 * site, said the old logo was "still" there, and asked explicitly for the full lockup — name and all — in
 * both header and footer. This is a different image now: `/brand/lrf-logo.png`, trimmed from the new
 * master's FULL ink box (monogram + rule + "LEONARD RIEKSEN" + "FOTOGRAFIE"), aspect-ratio 1000/414.
 *
 * SIZE, decided by legibility rather than by reusing the old bar. The pre-D-88 version of this component
 * DID show the full lockup inside the unchanged 4.75rem bar (9.75rem wide → 61.9px tall) — but at that
 * scale "FOTOGRAFIE" rendered at ~6px, present but not actually readable, closer to a texture than a word.
 * Given the owner's brief here is explicitly to show the name, that scale was rejected rather than reused.
 * Instead the box is sized off the SMALLER of the two name lines ("FOTOGRAFIE", 40px native): at a render
 * height of 5.5rem (88px) it lands at ~8.5px caps and "LEONARD RIEKSEN" at ~10.4px — small, but a tracked
 * all-caps line, which is the type of text most tolerant of small sizes. `--header-height` (tokens.css)
 * grew from 4.75rem to 6.5rem to hold that with the same ~8px top/bottom clearance D-88 used, and every
 * fixed element measured against the old bar (the mobile-nav panel's inset, the portfolio world-rail's top
 * offset) now reads the same token, so nothing can drift out of sync with it again.
 *
 * D-91 (2026-09-18, owner instruction, same day) — BAR SIZE WINS OVER LOCKUP LEGIBILITY. The owner tried
 * the 6.5rem bar and asked for the original 76px (4.75rem) back explicitly, nothing else changed.
 * `--header-height` (tokens.css) is back to 4.75rem — the mobile-nav panel and portfolio world-rail offset
 * follow automatically, being the same token. The logo has to fit inside that again: height 3.75rem (60px),
 * ~8px clearance top/bottom (same convention as D-88/D-84). At that scale "FOTOGRAFIE" is back to ~5.8px —
 * essentially the D-84 precedent this file argued against reusing under D-90 — but this time it's not a
 * substitute the agent chose to avoid a header redesign; it's what the owner asked for directly, after
 * seeing the larger bar and rejecting it on size, not on the logo being small. Full lockup construction
 * (asset, no-crop, no-stretch) is unchanged; only the box shrank.
 *
 * FOCUS. `.wordmark` is a link and the first focusable element in the chrome, so BackToTop moves focus
 * here after a jump (see BackToTop.astro). For a POINTER visitor that programmatic move used to paint the
 * global 2px focus ring around the logo — a blue box appearing because a mouse user pressed an unrelated
 * button. `data-focus-quiet` (scripts/quiet-focus.js) suppresses the ring for exactly that one focus and
 * releases on the next key press, so keyboard visitors keep a full, visible indicator. See base.css.
 */
interface Props {
	class?: string;
}
const { class: className } = Astro.props;
---

<a class:list={['wordmark', className]} href="/" aria-label="Leonard Rieksen Fotografie, naar de homepagina">
	<img
		class="wordmark__image"
		src="/brand/lrf-logo.png"
		alt=""
		width="1000"
		height="414"
		draggable="false"
		decoding="async"
	/>
</a>

<style>
	.wordmark {
		display: block;
		/* The artwork's own ratio, so the box can never be a shape the lockup does not have. */
		aspect-ratio: 1000 / 414;
		height: 3.75rem;
		width: auto;
		color: var(--color-text);
		line-height: 0;
	}
	.wordmark__image {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
		/* Never crop, never stretch — the two failure modes this component exists to make impossible. */
		object-fit: contain;
		user-select: none;
		-webkit-user-select: none;
		-webkit-user-drag: none;
		-webkit-touch-callout: none;
	}
	/* Very narrow phones: the lockup steps down rather than crowding the menu trigger. --header-height
	   stays the same at every width, so the extra clearance just grows a little on the smallest screens. */
	@media (max-width: 26rem) {
		.wordmark {
			height: 3.25rem;
		}
	}
</style>
